var e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"08-a1b2c3d4","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Multi-AZ deployments","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company deploys a three-tier web application across 3 AZs. The database tier uses RDS Aurora Multi-AZ. The application tier uses EC2 in an Auto Scaling group. After a load test, the team notices that 80% of requests are served by instances in us-east-1a. What is the likely cause?","options":{"A":"Auto Scaling groups always prefer instances in the first AZ alphabetically","B":"The ALB has cross-zone load balancing disabled, and the ALB node in us-east-1a receives more client DNS resolutions","C":"RDS Aurora only allows connections from us-east-1a instances for performance","D":"The security group only allows inbound traffic from us-east-1a"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ALB distributes traffic via DNS — clients resolve the ALB DNS name and connect to one of the ALB nodes (one per AZ). If DNS resolution disproportionately returns the us-east-1a node's IP (due to TTL caching or DNS resolver behavior), that node handles 80% of connections. With cross-zone load balancing disabled, each ALB node distributes traffic only to targets in its own AZ. Enabling cross-zone load balancing ensures even distribution regardless of which ALB node receives the connection.","distractors":{"A":"Auto Scaling groups launch instances in AZs based on the configured AZ list and rebalancing — they do not preferentially launch in the first AZ alphabetically.","C":"Aurora connections are made to the writer endpoint or reader endpoint DNS — neither restricts which AZ the connecting instance is in.","D":"Security groups allow or deny traffic by IP/port — they do not filter by AZ, and a security group misconfiguration would cause failures, not disproportionate distribution."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/disable-cross-zone.html","tags":["alb","cross-zone","load-balancing","multi-az","dns"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-a1b2c3d5","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Disaster recovery patterns","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company currently uses a 'backup and restore' DR strategy for their database (RPO: 24 hours, RTO: 4 hours). A new business requirement mandates RPO ≤ 15 minutes and RTO ≤ 1 hour. Which DR strategy meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Backup and restore — increase backup frequency to every 15 minutes","B":"Pilot light — keep a minimal version of the environment running in the DR region with database replication","C":"Warm standby — maintain a scaled-down but fully functional environment in the DR region","D":"Multi-site active-active — run full production capacity in two regions simultaneously"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A warm standby DR strategy maintains a scaled-down but fully functional version of the environment in the DR region. The database continuously replicates (meeting RPO ≤ 15 minutes with cross-region replication). During a failover, the DR environment is scaled up to full capacity (meeting RTO ≤ 1 hour). Pilot light (B) would need longer to provision most infrastructure, and full active-active (D) exceeds the requirements and costs significantly more.","distractors":{"A":"Backup and restore with 15-minute frequency would meet the RPO but RTO would still be hours (restore database, provision infrastructure, etc.) — exceeding the 1-hour RTO requirement.","B":"Pilot light keeps only the core database running — application servers need to be provisioned from scratch during failover, likely exceeding the 1-hour RTO for full recovery.","D":"Multi-site active-active meets both requirements but is the most expensive option — it is overkill when warm standby satisfies the business requirements at lower cost."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/disaster-recovery-workloads-on-aws/disaster-recovery-options-in-the-cloud.html","tags":["disaster-recovery","warm-standby","rto","rpo","multi-region"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-a1b2c3d6","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Kinesis and SQS","difficulty":"medium","question":"An e-commerce platform needs to process order events with guaranteed delivery order (FIFO) per customer, with multiple downstream consumers (analytics, inventory, shipping). Each consumer must receive ALL events independently. Which architecture satisfies both requirements?","options":{"A":"SQS FIFO Queue with 3 consumer applications — SQS FIFO guarantees order per MessageGroupId","B":"SNS FIFO Topic publishing to 3 SQS FIFO Queues (one per consumer) — provides per-customer ordering and fan-out","C":"SQS Standard Queue with 3 consumers — Standard Queue is fast and all consumers share the messages","D":"Kinesis Data Streams with 1 shard — all records in one shard are strictly ordered"},"correct":"B","explanation":"SNS FIFO + SQS FIFO fan-out: The SNS FIFO topic preserves message ordering and uses MessageGroupId (e.g., customerId) to guarantee FIFO per customer. Each SQS FIFO subscriber queue receives all messages independently (fan-out). Analytics, inventory, and shipping each consume from their own queue — independent processing, no message contention. This is the canonical pattern for ordered fan-out.","distractors":{"A":"A single SQS FIFO queue with 3 consumers means each message is received by only ONE consumer (SQS is a competing consumer model) — all three consumers cannot each receive all messages from the same queue.","C":"SQS Standard queue is not FIFO and delivers messages to competing consumers — neither requirement (FIFO per customer, all events to all consumers) is met.","D":"A single Kinesis shard provides strict ordering for all records but supports multiple consumers via Enhanced Fan-Out. However, a single shard has 1 MB/s write limit — may be insufficient at scale. The SNS FIFO + SQS FIFO pattern is more directly applicable and scalable."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/fifo-message-filtering.html","tags":["sqs","sns","fifo","fan-out","ordering","consumers"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-a1b2c3d7","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Aurora Global Database","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company uses Aurora Global Database with a primary cluster in us-east-1 and a secondary cluster in eu-west-1. The primary region experiences a complete regional outage. What is the correct failover process and what is the typical RTO?","options":{"A":"Aurora automatically detects the outage and fails over to eu-west-1 within 30 seconds with zero manual intervention","B":"Manually promote the secondary cluster in eu-west-1 to standalone using the 'Detach and Promote' process — typical RTO is under 1 minute for promoting the secondary","C":"Redirect all traffic to a Read Replica in eu-west-1 — it automatically accepts writes during regional outages","D":"Aurora Global Database includes automated cross-region failover — RTO is approximately 30 minutes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aurora Global Database does NOT automatically fail over to the secondary cluster — regional failover requires manual intervention. The process: (1) detach the secondary cluster from the global database, (2) promote it to a standalone primary cluster. This typically takes less than 1 minute for the promotion. The resulting RTO (including promotion + DNS update + application reconnection) is typically under 1 minute, achieving near-zero RPO due to <1 second replication lag. AWS is adding Managed Planned Failover but unplanned failover still requires manual steps.","distractors":{"A":"Aurora Global Database does NOT automatically fail over during regional outages — it requires manual promotion of the secondary cluster.","C":"Read Replicas in the secondary cluster are read-only by definition — they cannot accept writes until explicitly promoted. Promotion does not happen automatically.","D":"Aurora Global Database failover takes under 1 minute for promotion, not 30 minutes. Automatic failover is not available for unplanned regional outages."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/aurora-global-database-disaster-recovery.html","tags":["aurora","global-database","failover","rto","regional-outage"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-a2b3c4d5","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"ECS and containers","difficulty":"medium","question":"An ECS Fargate service runs 10 tasks. A deployment of a new container image should replace all tasks with zero downtime. The service uses an ALB. Which ECS deployment configuration ensures zero downtime during rolling updates?","options":{"A":"Set minimum healthy percent to 0% and maximum percent to 100% — terminate all old tasks before launching new ones","B":"Set minimum healthy percent to 100% and maximum percent to 200% — launch new tasks alongside old tasks before terminating old ones","C":"Use the ECS Blue/Green deployment type with CodeDeploy — traffic shifts from old to new task set","D":"Set minimum healthy percent to 50% — acceptable to be at 50% capacity during deployments"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ECS Blue/Green deployment with AWS CodeDeploy creates a new replacement task set (green), runs health checks, then shifts traffic from the old (blue) to new (green) task set using the ALB. You can configure linear, canary, or all-at-once traffic shifting. The old task set is not terminated until the new one is fully healthy and receiving traffic. This provides true zero downtime with the ability to roll back instantly by shifting traffic back to blue.","distractors":{"A":"Minimum 0% / maximum 100% is a 'recreate' strategy — all tasks are terminated before new ones launch, causing complete downtime. This is the worst option for zero downtime.","B":"Minimum 100% / maximum 200% is a valid rolling update strategy that maintains full capacity — new tasks launch (up to 200%), then old tasks terminate. This achieves zero downtime but does not offer traffic shifting control or instant rollback capability like Blue/Green.","D":"Minimum 50% allows the service to drop to 5 tasks during deployment — users experience degraded capacity (not true zero-downtime from a capacity perspective)."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECS/latest/developerguide/deployment-type-bluegreen.html","tags":["ecs","fargate","blue-green","deployment","zero-downtime","codedeploy"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-a2b3c4d6","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"S3 and storage","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company uploads millions of files per day to S3. An audit reveals that a significant number of PUT requests fail intermittently with 503 SlowDown errors. What is the cause and solution?","options":{"A":"S3 bucket is full — increase the bucket capacity limit","B":"Too many requests are targeting the same S3 prefix (hot prefix) — use randomized prefix naming to distribute I/O across S3 partitions","C":"S3 Standard does not support high request rates — migrate to S3 Express One Zone","D":"The IAM role lacks sufficient permissions for high-volume PUTs — add PutObject:* permission"},"correct":"B","explanation":"S3 automatically partitions storage by key prefix to handle high request rates. When millions of requests target the same prefix (e.g., \`uploads/2024/01/\`), a single S3 partition handles all requests, causing throttling (503 SlowDown). Adding random prefixes (hash or UUID prefix) distributes requests across S3 partitions — S3 can handle 3,500+ PUT/COPY/POST/DELETE and 5,500+ GET/HEAD requests per second per prefix partition. S3 now auto-partitions without requiring random prefixes for most workloads, but extremely high rates may still benefit from prefix distribution.","distractors":{"A":"S3 has no capacity limit — it scales to exabytes automatically. There is no per-bucket storage limit to hit.","C":"S3 Express One Zone is optimized for latency-sensitive workloads — it may help with raw throughput but random prefix naming is the standard solution for partition-based throttling.","D":"503 SlowDown errors are S3 service-side throttling, not IAM permission errors — an IAM issue would produce 403 Forbidden responses."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/optimizing-performance.html","tags":["s3","throttling","prefix","performance","partitions"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-b1c2d3e4","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"IAM and access control","difficulty":"medium","question":"A developer has the IAM policy below attached to their user. Can they create an S3 bucket named 'dev-bucket-123'?\\n\\n{\\"Effect\\":\\"Allow\\",\\"Action\\":\\"s3:CreateBucket\\",\\"Resource\\":\\"arn:aws:s3:::dev-*\\"}\\n\\nAnd can they delete a bucket named 'prod-bucket-456'?","options":{"A":"Can create dev-bucket-123 (matches dev-*); cannot delete prod-bucket-456 (no s3:DeleteBucket permission granted)","B":"Can create dev-bucket-123 AND can delete prod-bucket-456 — s3:* is implicitly granted","C":"Cannot create dev-bucket-123 — bucket names must include the account ID in the ARN","D":"Can create dev-bucket-123; can delete prod-bucket-456 because S3 bucket deletion is always allowed for the bucket owner"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The policy grants s3:CreateBucket on resources matching 'arn:aws:s3:::dev-*'. 'dev-bucket-123' matches 'dev-*', so CreateBucket is allowed. 'prod-bucket-456' does not match 'dev-*', so CreateBucket is denied there. More importantly, s3:DeleteBucket is not granted anywhere in this policy — the only allowed action is s3:CreateBucket. IAM uses implicit deny by default — no explicit allow means the action is denied.","distractors":{"B":"The policy only grants s3:CreateBucket — s3:* is NOT implicitly granted. IAM requires explicit allows for each action.","C":"S3 bucket ARNs in IAM policies use the format 'arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name' without an account ID — this is correct S3 ARN syntax.","D":"IAM policies apply regardless of resource ownership — the developer needs an explicit s3:DeleteBucket allow in an IAM policy to delete any bucket."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_evaluation-logic.html","tags":["iam","s3","policy","resource-arn","permissions"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-b1c2d3e5","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Encryption and KMS","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company uses AWS KMS customer-managed keys (CMKs) to encrypt RDS databases and S3 buckets. An employee with IAM AdministratorAccess accidentally runs \`aws kms delete-key\`. What happens?","options":{"A":"The CMK is immediately deleted and all encrypted data is permanently inaccessible","B":"KMS requires a 7 to 30 day waiting period (key pending deletion) before the CMK is deleted — the deletion can be cancelled during this window","C":"CMKs cannot be deleted — only disabled","D":"The key is deleted but KMS retains encrypted data keys for 90 days for recovery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS KMS has a mandatory minimum waiting period of 7 days (up to 30 days) between scheduling a CMK deletion and actual deletion. During this pending deletion window, the CMK is disabled (no encrypt/decrypt operations) but not yet deleted. If data encrypted with this key needs to be accessed, or if the deletion was accidental, you can cancel the scheduled deletion and re-enable the key. After the waiting period expires, the key is permanently deleted and data encrypted only with this key becomes unrecoverable.","distractors":{"A":"Immediate deletion is not possible — KMS enforces a mandatory waiting period of at least 7 days for exactly this reason (preventing accidental data loss).","C":"CMKs CAN be deleted after the mandatory waiting period — they can also be disabled (which prevents use but does not delete the key material).","D":"KMS does not retain encrypted data keys for recovery after CMK deletion — this is a fictional feature. Once the CMK is deleted, data keys encrypted with it cannot be decrypted."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/deleting-keys.html","tags":["kms","cmk","deletion","waiting-period","encryption"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-b1c2d3e6","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"VPC security","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company's application in a private subnet needs to call an external third-party HTTPS API at a fixed IP (203.0.113.50). The security team requires explicit allowlisting of this IP in egress rules. Currently, all internet egress goes through a NAT Gateway. What configuration controls outbound access to ONLY 203.0.113.50 on port 443?","options":{"A":"Update the Security Group outbound rule to allow TCP 443 to 203.0.113.50/32 only","B":"Update the NACL to deny all outbound TCP 443 except to 203.0.113.50/32","C":"Remove the NAT Gateway default route and add a specific host route for 203.0.113.50/32 via NAT Gateway","D":"Use AWS WAF to create an IP-based egress rule"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Security Groups support outbound rules specifying destination CIDR (203.0.113.50/32), port (443), and protocol (TCP). By default, security groups allow all outbound traffic — to restrict to only 203.0.113.50:443, remove the default 'allow all outbound' rule and add a specific rule allowing TCP 443 to 203.0.113.50/32. Security groups are stateful — allowing the outbound connection automatically allows the response traffic back in.","distractors":{"B":"NACLs are stateless — you'd also need to allow the inbound response traffic (ephemeral ports 1024-65535 from 203.0.113.50). While NACLs work at the subnet level, security groups at the instance level are the more precise and appropriate tool for this use case.","C":"Adding a specific host route for the third-party IP doesn't restrict other egress traffic — the existing default route (0.0.0.0/0 via NAT) still allows all other internet access. Removing the default route would block all other internet access, including legitimate outbound traffic.","D":"AWS WAF operates at Layer 7 for HTTP/HTTPS traffic on ALB, CloudFront, and API Gateway — it does not apply to outbound traffic from EC2 instances to external APIs."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/security-group-rules.html","tags":["security-groups","egress","outbound","allowlist","vpc"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-b1c2d3e7","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Inspector and Security Hub","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company runs 200 EC2 instances and wants automated discovery of OS-level vulnerabilities (unpatched CVEs) and network exposure (open ports accessible from the internet). Which service provides this assessment continuously?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"Amazon Inspector v2","C":"Amazon GuardDuty","D":"AWS Security Hub"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Inspector v2 provides automated, continuous vulnerability management for EC2 instances, ECR container images, and Lambda functions. It uses the SSM agent to scan EC2 instances for software vulnerabilities (CVEs from the NVD), unintended network exposure (reachability analysis from internet gateways), and package vulnerabilities. Inspector continuously reassesses when new CVEs are published or software is changed — not just point-in-time scans.","distractors":{"A":"Trusted Advisor checks security group configurations (unrestricted ports) but does not perform OS-level CVE scanning or deep network reachability analysis.","C":"GuardDuty detects behavioral threats from logs (unusual API calls, crypto mining) — it does not scan for software vulnerabilities or CVEs on EC2 instances.","D":"Security Hub aggregates and prioritizes findings from Inspector, GuardDuty, and other services — it is a management console, not a scanner. Inspector does the actual vulnerability scanning."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/inspector/latest/user/what-is-inspector.html","tags":["inspector","vulnerability","cve","ec2","security"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-b1c2d3e8","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Cognito and access management","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company has a REST API on API Gateway. Authenticated users should access all endpoints. Unauthenticated users should access only /public endpoints. The company uses Amazon Cognito User Pools. How should the API Gateway authorization be configured?","options":{"A":"Create one API Gateway authorizer for all routes — block unauthenticated users from all endpoints","B":"Configure a Cognito User Pool authorizer on all routes except /public routes — leave /public routes with no authorization (NONE)","C":"Use Lambda authorizers for all routes, returning allow/deny based on the path","D":"Enable API Gateway usage plans with two tiers: authenticated and unauthenticated"},"correct":"B","explanation":"API Gateway allows per-route authorization configuration. Set the Cognito User Pool authorizer on all protected routes (requiring a valid Cognito ID token). For /public routes, set the authorization type to NONE (no authorization required). This is the simplest, most direct implementation — Cognito authorizer validates JWTs for protected routes; public routes bypass authorization entirely. No custom Lambda code required.","distractors":{"A":"Configuring authorization on all routes blocks unauthenticated users from /public endpoints — violating the requirement for public accessibility without login.","C":"Lambda authorizers work but add cost, complexity, and latency compared to the native Cognito User Pool authorizer. When Cognito User Pools are already in use, the native authorizer is the right choice.","D":"Usage plans with API keys manage rate limits and quotas for different consumer tiers — they do not implement authentication or path-based access control."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/apigateway/latest/developerguide/apigateway-integrate-with-cognito.html","tags":["api-gateway","cognito","authorization","jwt","public-routes"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-b2c3d4e5","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"S3 and data protection","difficulty":"medium","question":"An S3 bucket is accidentally made public by a developer who adds a bucket policy allowing 's3:GetObject' for principal '*'. AWS detects this within minutes. Which AWS service automatically detected this misconfiguration?","options":{"A":"Amazon GuardDuty","B":"AWS Config with the 's3-bucket-public-read-prohibited' managed rule","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Config evaluates resource configurations against rules in near-real-time. The managed Config rule 's3-bucket-public-read-prohibited' continuously checks whether S3 bucket policies or ACLs grant public read access. When the developer adds the public policy, Config detects the change within minutes and marks the bucket as NON_COMPLIANT. Config can be configured to trigger automated remediation (e.g., Lambda to remove the public policy) via Config Remediation or EventBridge.","distractors":{"A":"GuardDuty detects behavioral threats from API logs (unusual access patterns, crypto mining) — it does not evaluate S3 bucket configuration compliance. However, GuardDuty does have an S3 protection feature that alerts on suspicious access patterns to public buckets.","C":"Inspector scans for OS vulnerabilities and network exposure — it does not evaluate S3 bucket policy configurations.","D":"Trusted Advisor does check for S3 bucket permissions, but AWS Config with managed rules provides more granular, near-real-time detection and automated remediation."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/config/latest/developerguide/s3-bucket-public-read-prohibited.html","tags":["config","s3","public-access","compliance","misconfiguration"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-b2c3d4e6","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"VPN and Direct Connect","difficulty":"medium","question":"A financial company needs a dedicated, private connection to AWS that does NOT traverse the public internet, with bandwidth of 10 Gbps. Additionally, all data transmitted must be encrypted in transit. Which combination achieves both requirements?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect only — it is private and encrypted","B":"AWS Direct Connect (10 Gbps) + MACsec encryption on the connection","C":"Site-to-Site VPN with 10 Gbps bandwidth allocation","D":"Direct Connect with VPN over the Direct Connect connection (VPN over DX)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private connection that does not traverse the internet — meeting the privacy requirement. However, Direct Connect is NOT encrypted by default (it is a private MPLS circuit, not encrypted). For encryption, AWS supports MACsec (IEEE 802.1AE) on Direct Connect dedicated connections (1 Gbps, 10 Gbps, 100 Gbps) — it encrypts traffic at Layer 2 on the dedicated link. MACsec + Direct Connect provides private + encrypted connectivity at 10 Gbps.","distractors":{"A":"Direct Connect is private but NOT encrypted by default — data is transmitted in plaintext on the dedicated fiber circuit. Many compliance frameworks require encryption in transit even on private circuits.","C":"Site-to-Site VPN provides encryption (IPsec) but traverses the public internet and is limited to ~1.25 Gbps per tunnel — it does not meet the 10 Gbps private circuit requirement.","D":"VPN over Direct Connect uses IPsec encryption over the DX private circuit — this works but has throughput limitations and overhead. MACsec is the modern preferred solution for Layer 2 encryption on Direct Connect."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/directconnect/latest/UserGuide/MACsec.html","tags":["direct-connect","macsec","encryption","private","10gbps"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-c1d2e3f4","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"API Gateway and Lambda","difficulty":"medium","question":"An API receives 100 requests per second, each triggering a Lambda function that queries RDS. RDS has 100 max connections. After a traffic spike to 500 RPS, the database throws 'too many connections' errors. What is the correct architectural fix?","options":{"A":"Increase Lambda reserved concurrency to 500","B":"Add RDS Proxy between Lambda and RDS to pool database connections","C":"Upgrade RDS to a larger instance with more max connections","D":"Enable API Gateway caching for all Lambda responses"},"correct":"B","explanation":"RDS Proxy pools and multiplexes Lambda connections to the database. Lambda at 500 RPS may create 500+ simultaneous connections — far exceeding RDS limits. RDS Proxy maintains a pool of database connections and shares them across Lambda invocations, allowing 500 Lambda functions to use a much smaller number of actual database connections (e.g., 10-20). This is the canonical solution for Lambda-to-RDS connection exhaustion without changing the RDS instance.","distractors":{"A":"Increasing Lambda concurrency would allow MORE simultaneous Lambda invocations, creating even MORE database connections — making the problem worse, not better.","C":"Upgrading to a larger instance increases the max_connections limit but not enough to support unlimited Lambda concurrency — Lambda can create thousands of connections during high traffic.","D":"API Gateway caching caches API responses at the gateway level — it reduces Lambda invocations for cacheable requests but does not help for database-heavy, non-cacheable requests."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/rds-proxy.html","tags":["rds-proxy","lambda","connections","api-gateway","connection-pooling"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-c1d2e3f5","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"ElastiCache and performance","difficulty":"hard","question":"An application uses a cache-aside pattern with ElastiCache Redis. On a cache miss, the application queries RDS, stores the result in Redis with a 10-minute TTL, and returns the response. During a major cache invalidation event (cache flush), all 1,000 users simultaneously experience cache misses and query RDS simultaneously. What anti-pattern is this, and how is it prevented?","options":{"A":"Cache stampede (thundering herd) — use probabilistic early expiration or mutex/lock pattern to prevent simultaneous cache rebuilds","B":"Cache poisoning — enable Redis AUTH to prevent unauthorized cache flushes","C":"Cache fragmentation — increase Redis memory to prevent eviction","D":"Hot key problem — use Redis cluster mode to distribute the load"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is a cache stampede (thundering herd): when a cached item expires or is invalidated, all concurrent requests simultaneously experience a cache miss, simultaneously query the backend, and simultaneously try to rebuild the cache — overloading the database. Solutions: (1) Probabilistic early expiration — recompute cache proactively before TTL expires (small random probability), (2) mutex/distributed lock — only one request rebuilds the cache; others wait, (3) cache warming — pre-populate cache before serving traffic after a flush.","distractors":{"B":"Cache poisoning is when malicious or incorrect data is written to the cache — it's a security/data integrity issue, not the concurrent miss problem described.","C":"Cache fragmentation occurs when Redis memory has many small allocated-but-unusable gaps — it is a memory management issue, not a concurrent request flooding issue.","D":"Hot key problem is when a single cache key receives disproportionate traffic — the thundering herd problem describes simultaneous misses across many keys, not a single hot key."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/red-ug/Strategies.html","tags":["elasticache","redis","cache-stampede","thundering-herd","cache-aside"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-c1d2e3f6","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"Redshift and analytics","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company runs complex analytics queries on a 10 TB Redshift cluster. Queries routinely scan entire large tables, and performance is slow. A data engineer notices that the 'bookings' fact table uses EVEN distribution but is frequently joined with the 'users' dimension table (5 GB) using userId. What optimization should be applied?","options":{"A":"Change bookings to KEY distribution on userId; change users to KEY distribution on userId","B":"Change users to ALL distribution; leave bookings on EVEN distribution","C":"Enable Redshift Spectrum to offload the join to S3","D":"Increase Redshift cluster nodes from dc2.large to ra3.xlplus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For large fact-to-dimension joins on the same column (userId), co-locating data on the same node using KEY distribution on the JOIN key is most efficient. Both tables distributed by KEY on userId ensure that userId=123 in bookings and userId=123 in users reside on the same node — no network shuffle needed during the join. For a 5 GB dimension table, ALL distribution (copy to every node) is also valid and simpler; KEY distribution on both is better when the dimension table is too large for ALL.","distractors":{"B":"ALL distribution for users (copy to all nodes) eliminates data movement during joins and is a valid option for smaller dimension tables (typically < 5 GB). For a 5 GB table, KEY distribution on userId for both tables is typically more efficient and uses less memory per node.","C":"Redshift Spectrum offloads queries to S3 data — the bookings table is already in Redshift. Moving data to S3 for Spectrum would add extraction overhead and does not solve the join performance problem.","D":"Upgrading nodes increases compute and memory but doesn't fix the data distribution inefficiency — the same expensive cross-node data shuffle occurs regardless of node size."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html","tags":["redshift","distribution","key","join","performance","optimization"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-c1d2e3f7","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"Lambda and event processing","difficulty":"hard","question":"A Lambda function processes financial transactions from an SQS queue. Processing is idempotent — running the same transaction twice has no harmful effect. The function occasionally times out after 15 minutes of processing a batch. Messages reappear in the queue after the visibility timeout and are reprocessed. The team wants to report processing metrics. What is the BEST approach to prevent duplicate processing of the entire batch when a function times out?","options":{"A":"Increase the Lambda timeout to 30 minutes","B":"Use SQS partial batch response (reportBatchItemFailures) — Lambda reports which individual messages succeeded vs. failed; only failed messages are retried","C":"Enable FIFO queue to prevent out-of-order processing","D":"Reduce batch size to 1 message per Lambda invocation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"SQS partial batch response (reportBatchItemFailures) allows Lambda to report the subset of messages that failed within a batch. Instead of retrying the entire batch when one message fails (or the function times out), only the specific failed messages are retried. This prevents successfully processed messages from being processed again when a batch partially fails due to timeouts or individual message errors — dramatically reducing duplicate processing.","distractors":{"A":"Lambda's maximum timeout is 15 minutes — you cannot set it to 30 minutes. Additionally, increasing the timeout doesn't fix the root cause (partial batch failures causing full-batch retries).","C":"FIFO queues provide ordered processing and exactly-once delivery but do not prevent batch-level retries when Lambda times out — they address ordering, not partial failure handling.","D":"Reducing batch size to 1 eliminates batch partial failure, but it dramatically reduces throughput (one Lambda invocation per message) and increases costs — partial batch response is more efficient."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-sqs.html#services-sqs-batchfailurereporting","tags":["lambda","sqs","partial-batch","idempotent","retry"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-c2d3e4f5","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"Global Accelerator and networking","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company has an NLB in us-east-1 behind Global Accelerator. They want to add a second NLB in eu-west-1 and route European users to the European endpoint while routing North American users to the US endpoint. How does Global Accelerator implement this?","options":{"A":"Create two separate accelerators — one per endpoint","B":"Add the eu-west-1 NLB as a second endpoint in the Global Accelerator endpoint group; Global Accelerator automatically routes each user to the nearest (lowest latency) endpoint","C":"Use Route 53 latency-based routing to distribute between the two Global Accelerator IP addresses","D":"Create a new endpoint group for eu-west-1 in the same accelerator; Global Accelerator routes users based on the endpoint group's traffic dial and user location"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Global Accelerator organizes endpoints into endpoint groups by AWS region. Each endpoint group has a traffic dial (0-100%) controlling what fraction of traffic routed to that region. For geographic routing, create an endpoint group for us-east-1 and another for eu-west-1. Global Accelerator routes each user to the endpoint group with the nearest AWS edge location, then uses the traffic dial to control the proportion. Users naturally route to the nearest region's endpoint group.","distractors":{"A":"A single accelerator can have multiple endpoint groups and endpoints — there is no need for separate accelerators.","B":"A single endpoint group contains endpoints in the same region — to have endpoints in both us-east-1 and eu-west-1, you need endpoint groups for each region. Within a single endpoint group, Global Accelerator routes to the lowest-latency endpoint within that group.","C":"Global Accelerator uses two static anycast IPs — routing is handled by Global Accelerator's anycast infrastructure, not by Route 53. Route 53 is not used to route between Global Accelerator IPs."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/global-accelerator/latest/dg/about-endpoint-groups.html","tags":["global-accelerator","endpoint-groups","multi-region","traffic-dial","routing"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-c2d3e4f6","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"Storage performance","difficulty":"medium","question":"A media processing application needs to read a single 100 GB file sequentially at the highest possible throughput. The file is read once per day. Which storage configuration maximizes sequential read throughput?","options":{"A":"EBS gp3 volume with maximum provisioned IOPS (16,000) and throughput (1,000 MB/s)","B":"S3 with multiple parallel byte-range GET requests using the Range header","C":"EFS with Max I/O performance mode and Provisioned Throughput","D":"EC2 instance store NVMe SSD (local storage)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"S3 parallel byte-range GETs allow splitting the 100 GB file into multiple chunks (e.g., 100 × 1 GB ranges) and downloading all chunks simultaneously using multiple threads or connections. Each individual range request gets up to 5 GB/s S3 throughput, and running 100 parallel requests can achieve aggregate speeds of tens of GB/s — far exceeding any single EBS or EFS volume. This technique is used by AWS SDK transfer acceleration and is documented as an S3 performance best practice.","distractors":{"A":"EBS gp3 maximum throughput is 1,000 MB/s — significantly less than what S3 parallel range GETs can achieve. For a single sequential read of a large file, S3 parallel access outperforms a single EBS volume.","C":"EFS Provisioned Throughput can be set to high levels, but at significant cost — and EFS per-client throughput is limited. For a single sequential read, S3 parallel range GETs are faster and cheaper.","D":"Instance store NVMe provides excellent performance but the data must first be on the instance — if the file is read once per day and stored in S3, copying to instance store then reading adds an extra step and the instance store data is lost when the instance stops."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/optimizing-performance-guidelines.html","tags":["s3","parallel","byte-range","throughput","sequential-read"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-d1e2f3g4","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Cost-Optimized Architectures","topic":"EC2 and compute cost","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company runs 50 EC2 instances on On-Demand pricing. AWS Compute Optimizer recommends downsizing 30 instances (saving $2,000/month) and rightsizing 10 instances to Graviton3 (saving $800/month). The remaining 10 instances are optimally sized. After rightsizing, what is the NEXT cost optimization step?","options":{"A":"Purchase Reserved Instances for all 50 instances immediately","B":"Purchase Savings Plans or Reserved Instances for the newly rightsized instances to lock in commitment discounts on top of the rightsizing savings","C":"Enable auto-scaling on all instances to scale down when idle","D":"Enable Hibernate on all instances to stop billing when idle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The optimization sequence is: (1) rightsize first (Compute Optimizer recommendations), THEN (2) commit to RIs or Savings Plans for the right-sized fleet. Committing before rightsizing wastes the commitment on over-provisioned instances. After rightsizing to the correct instance types and sizes, purchase Savings Plans or RIs for the stable baseline workload — stacking rightsizing savings (20-40%) on top of RI/SP discounts (40-72%) maximizes total savings.","distractors":{"A":"Purchasing RIs before rightsizing commits to the wrong (over-provisioned) instance types — you'd be paying for RI discounts on instances you're about to downsize, potentially wasting the commitment.","C":"Auto-scaling is for dynamically scaling based on demand — it doesn't directly address the cost of correctly sized instances running at appropriate utilization. Auto-scaling + proper rightsizing + RIs are complementary strategies.","D":"Hibernate saves instance memory state to EBS and stops billing for instance hours — it is for temporarily stopping instances, not for persistent cost optimization of always-running instances."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/compute-optimizer/latest/ug/what-is-compute-optimizer.html","tags":["compute-optimizer","rightsizing","savings-plans","cost-optimization","sequence"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-d1e2f3g5","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Cost-Optimized Architectures","topic":"Database cost optimization","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company runs an Aurora MySQL cluster with 1 writer and 3 reader instances to handle read traffic. Analysis shows reader CPU utilization averages 5% and the readers handle fewer than 100 queries per minute. What is the most cost-effective change?","options":{"A":"Upgrade the writer to a larger instance to handle all reads on the primary","B":"Remove 2 reader instances and convert to Aurora Serverless v2 readers that scale to 0.5 ACU during low-usage periods","C":"Migrate all readers to DynamoDB for lower cost","D":"Enable Aurora read replica auto-scaling to maintain 3 readers at all times"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Three constantly running Aurora reader instances at 5% CPU utilization are massively over-provisioned. Converting to Aurora Serverless v2 readers allows the readers to scale down to 0.5 ACU (the minimum) during low-usage periods — paying only for the compute consumed. Serverless v2 scales up automatically if needed. Alternatively, reducing the number of readers from 3 to 1 provisioned reader significantly reduces cost while still providing read scale for the 100 queries/minute.","distractors":{"A":"Routing all reads to the writer adds load to the primary and eliminates read scaling — it also violates the principle of separating read and write workloads and could impact write performance.","C":"Migrating readers to DynamoDB requires rewriting the application for a NoSQL data model — this is an extreme architectural change, not a cost optimization for an existing relational database.","D":"Aurora read replica auto-scaling maintains readers based on CPU/connection thresholds — at 5% CPU, the minimum count (1 or 2) may still be more than needed. Serverless v2 addresses this more efficiently."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/aurora-serverless-v2.html","tags":["aurora","serverless-v2","cost","readers","rightsizing"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-d1e2f3g6","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Cost-Optimized Architectures","topic":"Lambda and serverless cost","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company's Lambda function uses 10 GB of memory but profiling shows it only uses 2 GB during execution. The function runs for 3 seconds, 5 million times per month. What is the cost impact of reducing memory from 10 GB to 2 GB?","options":{"A":"Cost increases — more memory enables faster execution","B":"Cost decreases by 80% — Lambda charges per GB-second, so 2 GB × 3s × 5M = 30M GB-seconds vs. 10 GB × 3s × 5M = 150M GB-seconds","C":"Cost stays the same — Lambda rounds to the nearest GB allocation","D":"Cost decreases by 80%, but execution time will increase proportionally to offset savings"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lambda charges per GB-second of duration. At 10 GB: 10 × 3 × 5,000,000 = 150,000,000 GB-seconds × $0.0000166667 = $2,500/month. At 2 GB: 2 × 3 × 5,000,000 = 30,000,000 GB-seconds × $0.0000166667 = $500/month. Savings: $2,000/month (80% reduction). If the function only uses 2 GB of memory, reducing the allocation does not affect execution time (the CPU quota is proportional to memory, but 2 GB still provides enough CPU for a memory-bound 2 GB function).","distractors":{"A":"Reducing memory reduces the GB-second cost — it does not increase cost. More memory means more cost if execution time stays the same.","C":"Lambda memory is configured in 1 MB increments (64 MB minimum) — there is no 'round to nearest GB' pricing. Lambda charges for the exact configured memory.","D":"If the function only uses 2 GB of memory, reducing from 10 GB to 2 GB does not increase execution time — the function was not compute-bound at 10 GB (it only used 2 GB). Duration would stay at ~3 seconds."},"source_url":"https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/pricing/","tags":["lambda","memory","cost","gb-seconds","rightsizing"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-d1e2f3g7","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Cost-Optimized Architectures","topic":"S3 and data lifecycle","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company has 500 TB of data in S3 Standard. A lifecycle analysis shows: 30% is accessed weekly, 20% is accessed monthly, 50% has not been accessed in 6 months. The company wants to minimize costs. Which lifecycle configuration optimizes cost?","options":{"A":"Move all data to S3 Intelligent-Tiering immediately","B":"Configure lifecycle rules: transition data to Standard-IA after 30 days, to Glacier Instant Retrieval after 90 days, to Glacier Deep Archive after 180 days","C":"Move 50% of unaccessed data to Glacier Deep Archive immediately; leave frequently accessed data in Standard","D":"Enable S3 Intelligent-Tiering for all data and enable Archive Access tiers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"S3 Intelligent-Tiering with Archive Access tiers enabled automatically moves objects to the lowest-cost tier based on actual access patterns: Frequent Access (< 30 days idle), Infrequent Access (30-90 days idle), Archive Instant Access (90-180 days idle at millisecond retrieval), and Archive Access (180+ days idle at hours retrieval). For 50% of data not accessed in 6 months, it will land in Archive Access (near Glacier Instant pricing). The 30% weekly and 20% monthly data stays in appropriate active tiers. No access pattern guessing required.","distractors":{"A":"Moving to Intelligent-Tiering without enabling Archive Access tiers only uses Frequent and Infrequent tiers — the 50% unaccessed data stays in Infrequent Access rather than dropping to cheaper Archive Access tiers.","B":"Static lifecycle rules risk charging retrieval fees when unexpectedly accessed data triggers retrievals from Glacier. For mixed access patterns, Intelligent-Tiering adapts to actual usage without retrieval fees (within tiers that support millisecond access).","C":"Manually identifying the 50% unaccessed data requires operational effort and may misclassify data. Intelligent-Tiering automates this classification with no retrieval fees."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intelligent-tiering-overview.html","tags":["s3","intelligent-tiering","lifecycle","archive","cost"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-e1f2g3h4","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Step Functions and workflows","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company runs a multi-step order processing workflow: validate → charge → fulfill → notify. If the 'charge' step fails, the 'validate' step results must be rolled back. The workflow must reliably complete even if individual steps fail transiently. Which service orchestrates this with built-in retry and compensation logic?","options":{"A":"AWS SQS with multiple Lambda functions connected in sequence","B":"AWS Step Functions Standard Workflow with retry configuration and Catch/compensating transactions","C":"Amazon EventBridge rule chaining Lambda functions","D":"AWS Lambda calling each step synchronously in sequence"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Step Functions Standard Workflows orchestrate multi-step processes with built-in retry (with exponential backoff), error catching, and branching. Catch states enable compensation (saga pattern) — if 'charge' fails after retries, the workflow catches the error and executes a compensation step to roll back 'validate'. Step Functions maintains durable execution state, so workflow progress is preserved across failures and retries — critical for long-running business transactions.","distractors":{"A":"SQS-connected Lambda functions are loosely coupled — there is no built-in saga/compensation mechanism, no workflow state visibility, and transient failures require custom retry logic in each function.","C":"EventBridge rules trigger on events but do not maintain workflow state or provide built-in retry/compensation orchestration — events fire-and-forget with no tracking of multi-step progress.","D":"Synchronous Lambda-calling-Lambda creates a tight coupling and hits Lambda's 15-minute timeout for long workflows. There is no durable state between calls, making failure recovery complex."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-error-handling.html","tags":["step-functions","workflow","saga","retry","compensation","resilience"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-e1f2g3h5","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"IAM best practices","difficulty":"easy","question":"A company's AWS root account has an access key (Access Key ID + Secret Access Key) that was created by a former employee and is still active. What should the company do immediately?","options":{"A":"Rotate the root access key by creating a new one first","B":"Delete the root account access key — root account should never have access keys","C":"Change the root account password to invalidate the access key","D":"Enable MFA on the access key for additional security"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS best practice explicitly states: do not create or use root account access keys. The root account has unrestricted access to all AWS resources and cannot be restricted by IAM policies. If a root access key exists, it should be deleted immediately — not rotated, because a rotated key is still a root access key. Use individual IAM users/roles with least-privilege policies for programmatic access. The root account should only be used for specific tasks that require root access (e.g., changing support plan, closing account).","distractors":{"A":"Rotating (creating a new key, then deleting the old) still leaves a root access key in existence — a new root key is equally dangerous. The key should be deleted entirely, not rotated.","C":"Changing the password does not affect access keys — access keys and passwords are independent credentials. The access key remains active after a password change.","D":"MFA cannot be added to an access key — MFA is enabled on the user account (console access). An MFA-protected account still has the access key active and usable via API."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html","tags":["iam","root-account","access-keys","security","best-practices"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-e1f2g3h6","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"ECS and Fargate scaling","difficulty":"hard","question":"An ECS Fargate service processes user requests. During peak hours, CPU utilization reaches 95% and new tasks take 90 seconds to start (pulling a 2 GB container image). Users experience elevated latency during the scale-up period. How can scale-up latency be reduced without pre-warming instances?","options":{"A":"Use larger Fargate task sizes to reduce the number of tasks needed","B":"Store the container image in Amazon ECR in the same region and enable ECR image pull caching; also configure ECS to scale out earlier (lower CPU threshold)","C":"Switch from Fargate to EC2 launch type — EC2 instances start faster","D":"Use Lambda instead of ECS for request processing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Two optimizations: (1) ECR image caching — ECR stores images in layers; when the same base layers are already cached, pull time decreases significantly. Images already present in the ECR in the same region pull faster than from Docker Hub. Fargate maintains a layer cache, so subsequent task starts with the same image are faster. (2) Scale out earlier — trigger scaling at 60% CPU instead of 95% to allow 90 seconds for new tasks to start before utilization becomes critical.","distractors":{"A":"Larger Fargate tasks mean fewer tasks, but each task still needs 90 seconds to start — the scale-up latency problem remains. Fewer, larger tasks may actually make the latency worse if one task failure removes more capacity.","C":"EC2 launch type can start containers faster (if instances are already running) but still pulls the container image — image pull time is the bottleneck, not the compute layer.","D":"Switching to Lambda changes the execution model entirely — Lambda has different timeout limits (15 min), concurrency models, and cannot run arbitrary container workloads without rewriting the application."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECS/latest/developerguide/task_definition_parameters.html","tags":["ecs","fargate","ecr","scaling","image-pull","latency"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-e1f2g3h7","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Cost-Optimized Architectures","topic":"Networking costs","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company processes data in us-east-1 and stores results in S3. Their monthly bill shows $3,000 in S3 data transfer OUT costs. Investigation reveals Lambda functions are fetching 100 TB from S3 each month for processing. Lambda and S3 are in the same region. Why are there transfer costs, and what eliminates them?","options":{"A":"Lambda to S3 in the same region is always free — the $3,000 must be from another source","B":"Transfer from S3 to Lambda within the same region is free; the costs are from Lambda results being transferred OUT to the internet or another region","C":"Lambda functions incur data transfer costs when reading from S3 — use S3 Select to reduce data volume","D":"Use VPC endpoints for S3 to eliminate the data transfer charges within the same region"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Data transfer between Lambda and S3 within the same AWS region is free (no data transfer charges). The $3,000 in data transfer OUT costs must originate from a different source: S3 data transferred to the internet, to another region, or through CloudFront. Investigate the Cost Explorer breakdown by data transfer type (S3 → Internet, S3 → CloudFront, S3 → other region) to identify the actual source. Lambda-to-S3 in the same region is a free data path.","distractors":{"A":"While Lambda-to-S3 in the same region IS free, the question asks to identify where the $3,000 actually comes from — dismissing it entirely without investigating is incorrect.","C":"Lambda reading from S3 in the same region is free — S3 Select reduces the data volume returned (and thus processing time) but does not eliminate a non-existent transfer charge.","D":"An S3 VPC Gateway Endpoint routes S3 traffic privately through VPC (no NAT) — but Lambda in the same region already has free data transfer to S3, so a VPC endpoint doesn't change the cost for intra-region Lambda-to-S3 access."},"source_url":"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/pricing/","tags":["s3","data-transfer","lambda","cost","same-region"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-f1g2h3i4","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"CloudFront and resilience","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company's CloudFront distribution has an ALB origin in us-east-1. The ALB goes down. CloudFront returns errors to users. To improve resilience, the architect wants CloudFront to automatically failover to a secondary S3 static website origin. Which CloudFront feature implements origin failover?","options":{"A":"CloudFront Route 53 health checks routing to a backup origin","B":"CloudFront Origin Groups with a primary and failover origin — CloudFront automatically tries the failover when the primary returns 5xx errors","C":"CloudFront Lambda@Edge function detecting 5xx and redirecting to S3","D":"Two CloudFront distributions — Route 53 failover between them"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CloudFront Origin Groups allow configuring a primary origin (ALB) and a failover origin (S3) within the same distribution. If the primary origin returns specified HTTP error codes (4xx or 5xx, configurable) or times out, CloudFront automatically retries the request to the failover origin without the user experiencing the error. This provides automatic origin-level failover within a single CloudFront distribution.","distractors":{"A":"CloudFront itself does not integrate with Route 53 health checks for origin selection — Origin Groups provide origin failover natively within CloudFront.","C":"Lambda@Edge can detect errors and make decisions, but it is more complex and adds latency versus the native Origin Groups feature. Origin Groups is the purpose-built solution.","D":"Two distributions and Route 53 failover works but is complex — two distributions, two DNS records, health checks, and Route 53 failover routing all need configuration. Origin Groups within a single distribution is simpler."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/high_availability_origin_failover.html","tags":["cloudfront","origin-groups","failover","resilience","s3"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-f1g2h3i5","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"DynamoDB advanced","difficulty":"hard","question":"A DynamoDB table stores IoT sensor readings with \`deviceId\` (partition key) and \`readingTime\` (sort key). A query needs to find all readings for deviceId='device-001' between two timestamps. The query returns 10,000 items but only 50 items are between the timestamps. Which query is most efficient?","options":{"A":"Scan the entire table with a FilterExpression on deviceId and readingTime","B":"Query with KeyConditionExpression: deviceId='device-001' AND readingTime BETWEEN t1 AND t2","C":"Query with KeyConditionExpression: deviceId='device-001', then apply FilterExpression on readingTime","D":"Create a GSI with readingTime as partition key and query by time range"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Using BETWEEN in the KeyConditionExpression (not FilterExpression) limits data read at the DynamoDB partition level — DynamoDB reads only items in the specified time range for device-001, consuming RCUs only for those 50 items. This is the most efficient query: the key condition is applied during the read, not after. Option C (query all 10,000 items, then filter) reads all 10,000 items and consumes RCUs for all of them before filtering.","distractors":{"A":"A full table scan reads every item in the table, not just device-001's items — this is maximally inefficient for a known partition key query.","C":"Querying by deviceId='device-001' with a FilterExpression on readingTime reads ALL 10,000 items for device-001 (consuming full RCUs) then discards 9,950. Using BETWEEN in the KeyConditionExpression reads only the 50 items in range.","D":"A GSI with readingTime as partition key groups ALL devices' readings by time — it cannot efficiently query by deviceId within a time range without also filtering by deviceId, which is a FilterExpression on the GSI."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Query.KeyConditionExpressions.html","tags":["dynamodb","query","between","key-condition","rcu","efficiency"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-f1g2h3i6","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Macie and data security","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company stores millions of files in S3 across 30 buckets. The security team suspects some buckets may contain unencrypted PII (social security numbers, credit card numbers) that was uploaded by mistake. Which service automatically discovers and classifies sensitive data in S3?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector — scans S3 objects for PII","B":"Amazon Macie — uses ML to discover and classify sensitive data in S3","C":"AWS Config — evaluates S3 objects for PII compliance","D":"Amazon GuardDuty — detects PII in S3 bucket access patterns"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Macie uses machine learning and pattern matching to automatically discover and classify sensitive data in S3 — including PII (SSNs, credit card numbers, passport numbers), financial information, healthcare data, and credentials. Macie evaluates S3 objects across multiple buckets, generating findings that identify which buckets and objects contain sensitive data. This is the purpose-built AWS service for S3 data classification and PII discovery.","distractors":{"A":"Amazon Inspector scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities — it does not scan S3 object content for PII or sensitive data patterns.","C":"AWS Config evaluates S3 bucket configurations (encryption settings, public access, logging) — it does not inspect the content of S3 objects to find PII.","D":"GuardDuty analyzes behavioral patterns (unusual access, exfiltration attempts) in CloudTrail and VPC Flow Logs — it does not scan S3 object content to classify PII."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/macie/latest/user/what-is-macie.html","tags":["macie","pii","s3","data-classification","sensitive-data"],"verified":true},{"id":"08-f1g2h3i7","version":1,"setId":"set-08","domain":"Cost-Optimized Architectures","topic":"Reserved capacity and commitments","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company has 200 RDS PostgreSQL instances running 24/7. AWS Cost Explorer shows they have 40% Reserved Instance coverage (80 RIs, 120 On-Demand). The company wants to increase RI coverage to 80% while maintaining flexibility. What approach achieves this without over-committing?","options":{"A":"Purchase 80 more Standard Reserved Instances for the same instance type as the On-Demand instances","B":"Analyze actual usage with Cost Explorer RI recommendations, purchase RIs for stable workloads, keep 20% On-Demand for variable/experimental databases","C":"Purchase Convertible Reserved Instances for all 120 On-Demand instances","D":"Purchase 1-year RIs for all 120 On-Demand instances to reach 100% coverage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cost Explorer RI recommendations analyze 7-60 days of usage and recommend specific RI purchases (instance type, term, payment option) to maximize savings based on actual usage patterns. Purchase RIs for the stable 80% of databases (those running consistently). Keep 20% On-Demand for databases that may be terminated, changed, or are experimental. This achieves 80% coverage without over-committing on instances that may not run for the full RI term.","distractors":{"A":"Purchasing Standard RIs for the same instance type works but lacks flexibility analysis — Cost Explorer recommendations provide better insights on which specific instances to commit to.","C":"Purchasing Convertible RIs for all 120 On-Demand instances reaches 100% coverage but over-commits — some databases may be terminated or resized, wasting the RI commitment (though Convertible RIs allow some exchange flexibility).","D":"100% RI coverage (200 RIs) is unnecessarily aggressive — experimental or short-lived databases on RIs waste the commitment. 80% coverage balances savings with flexibility."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cost-management/latest/userguide/ri-recommendations.html","tags":["rds","reserved-instances","cost-explorer","coverage","commitment"],"verified":true}]`);export{e as default};